Say. Tutor only. Use these notes after the student finishes.
Ask. Agree or disagree: More pieces always means more muffin. Use the same whole to support your answer. Partner compare: How does a benchmark comparison differ from renaming both fractions?
Expect. P1: {3/7} < {5/7}
P2: {4/5} > {4/9}
P3: Less than {1/2}
P4: {3/5} > {7/12}
P5: Left to right: 0.5, {5/4}, 1 {3/4}
P6: {7/10} > 0.68
P7: One fourth of the way from 2 to 4
P8: {3/4} of a muffin
Exit: {7/10}, 0.72, {3/4}
Diagnostic 35 + break 5
Talk 5 · lesson 25 · exit 5
If stuck. Got it: Orders all three correctly and places them in order between 0.7 and 0.8, with {3/4} at 0.75. Almost there: Order is correct, but a position or label is imprecise; explains that larger denominator means smaller equal parts. Needs re-teaching: Reverses the fractions because 10 exceeds 4, or uses inconsistent units.
